From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: fall back to software csum/GSO
 on DSA conduits

The EDMA TX path enables the PPE inline checksum engine by setting the
ip_csum_en/l4_csum_en bits in TX descriptor word5 (edma_tx_fill_desc()),
but never programs the L3/L4 offset fields in word7 and ignores the
skb's csum_start/csum_offset. The hardware therefore locates the L3/L4
headers with its own packet parser, which only understands standard
Ethernet (optionally VLAN-tagged) framing.

When an EDMA port acts as a DSA conduit, the tagger (for example
DSA_TAG_PROTO_RTL8_4 used by RTL837x switches) inserts extra bytes
between the source MAC address and the EtherType. The parser then
miscomputes the header offsets and the hardware computes/inserts the L4
checksum at the wrong offsets, so every CHECKSUM_PARTIAL TCP/UDP frame
leaves the wire with a corrupted checksum and is dropped by the peer
(ICMP is unaffected as it is never offloaded). Disabling TX checksum
offload with "ethtool -K <conduit> tx off" makes the problem disappear,
confirming the diagnosis.

Documentation/networking/dsa/dsa.rst explicitly requires conduit
drivers whose offload engine does not understand the switch tag in use
to "correctly fall back to software checksum when the IP header is not
where the hardware expects".

Do that in ndo_features_check: when the netdevice is used as a DSA
conduit, mask out the checksum and GSO features so the core computes
the checksum (and segments GSO skbs) in software before handing the
frame to edma_port_xmit(). Ports not used as DSA conduits keep full
checksum/TSO offload.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_port.c | 15 ++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_port.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_port.c
@@ -10,6 +10,8 @@
 #include <linux/of_net.h>
 #include <linux/phylink.h>
 #include <linux/printk.h>
+
+#include <net/dsa.h>
 
 #include "edma.h"
 #include "edma_cfg_rx.h"
@@ -178,9 +180,22 @@ static int edma_port_change_mtu(struct n
 }
 
 static netdev_features_t edma_port_feature_check(__maybe_unused struct sk_buff *skb,
-						 __maybe_unused struct net_device *netdev,
+						 struct net_device *netdev,
 						 netdev_features_t features)
 {
+	/* The PPE TX checksum/TSO engine locates the L3/L4 headers with
+	 * an inline packet parser that only understands standard Ethernet
+	 * (optionally VLAN) framing: no L3/L4 offsets are programmed into
+	 * the TX descriptor and csum_start/csum_offset are ignored. When
+	 * this port is a DSA conduit, the tagger (e.g. RTL8_4) inserts
+	 * extra bytes between the source MAC and the EtherType, the parser
+	 * miscomputes the header offsets and the hardware corrupts the L4
+	 * checksum of every offloaded frame. Fall back to software
+	 * checksumming and GSO for DSA-tagged traffic.
+	 */
+	if (netdev_uses_dsa(netdev))
+		features &= ~(NETIF_F_CSUM_MASK | NETIF_F_GSO_MASK);
+
 	return features;
 }
 
